      "content": "Critics rely heavily on contractarian legal theory, asserting that slavery was a specific breach of contract that expired with the death of the original parties, thereby denying any legal validity for transferring liability to unrelated descendants. They argue that imposing obligations on those who never benefited from the injustice would constitute \"collective guilt,\" which fundamentally contradicts the legal and moral principle of individual responsibility. Furthermore, opponents often cite the Fourteenth Amendment, specifically the due process clause, to claim that compensation policies could unconstitutionally alter property rights without a direct, compensable claim.",
